# Week 2 — Baseline CNN Model and Training

## Completed This Week

During the second week, I focused on building and training the first deep learning model for the scene classification project.

The following tasks were completed:

* Connected Google Drive to Google Colab
* Loaded and prepared the Intel Image Classification dataset
* Split the dataset into training and validation subsets
* Applied image preprocessing and normalization
* Built a baseline Convolutional Neural Network (CNN)
* Trained the model for 5 epochs
* Evaluated model performance using validation accuracy
* Generated accuracy plots for training visualization
* Added classification metrics and confusion matrix evaluation

---

## Dataset Preparation

The dataset contains 6 scene categories:

* buildings
* forest
* glacier
* mountain
* sea
* street

Images were resized to 150×150 pixels and normalized to improve training stability.

The training dataset was loaded from:

```text id="ds1"
/content/data/seg_train
```

Validation data was created using an 80/20 split from the training set.

---

## Baseline CNN Architecture

A simple CNN baseline model was implemented using TensorFlow/Keras.

Model structure:

* Conv2D (32 filters)
* MaxPooling2D
* Conv2D (64 filters)
* MaxPooling2D
* Conv2D (128 filters)
* MaxPooling2D
* Flatten layer
* Dense layer (128 units)
* Output layer with Softmax activation

The model was compiled with:

* Optimizer: Adam
* Loss function: Sparse Categorical Crossentropy
* Metric: Accuracy

---

## Training Results

The model was trained for 5 epochs.

Final results:

| Metric              | Result  |
| ------------------- | ------- |
| Training Accuracy   | ~92%    |
| Validation Accuracy | ~78–80% |

The model learned meaningful visual features from the dataset and achieved good baseline performance for scene classification.

An accuracy graph was generated to visualize the learning process.

---

## Additional Evaluation

Additional evaluation methods were added:

* Classification Report
* Confusion Matrix
* Validation Accuracy Curves

These metrics helped analyze model behavior across different scene classes.

---

## Problems and Challenges

Some overfitting was observed during training because training accuracy became significantly higher than validation accuracy.

Possible reasons:

* limited model regularization,
* relatively small CNN architecture,
* no data augmentation yet.

Training also required significant GPU time in Google Colab.

